  <p>
    Polycystic ovary syndrome is a hormonal and metabolic condition, and four of its most
    recognisable features are on the skin. Often they are what brings someone to a dermatologist
    before PCOS has been diagnosed at all.
  </p>

  <h2>The four skin signs</h2>

  <h3>Acne along the jawline and chin</h3>
  <p>
    Deeper, more inflammatory lesions concentrated on the lower face, typically worse before
    periods, and often persisting well past the teenage years. The distribution is characteristic
    enough to raise the question on its own.
  </p>

  <h3>Hirsutism — excess facial and body hair</h3>
  <p>
    Coarse dark hair on the upper lip, chin, jawline, chest and abdomen, following a male pattern
    of distribution. One of the most distressing features, and one of the most treatable.
  </p>

  <h3>Scalp hair thinning</h3>
  <p>
    Diffuse loss of density over the crown, with the parting widening. Driven by the same
    androgen excess that causes hair growth elsewhere.
  </p>

  <h3>Acanthosis nigricans</h3>
  <p>
    Velvety dark thickening in the neck folds, armpits and groin. It is a visible sign of insulin
    resistance rather than a pigmentation problem, which is why bleaching creams do so little for
    it.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is assessed</h2>

  <p>
    History covers cycle regularity, when the skin changes started and how quickly they
    progressed, weight change, and family history. Examination documents the pattern and extent
    of hair growth, the acne distribution, scalp density on trichoscopy, and the presence of
    acanthosis nigricans.
  </p>

  <p>
    Blood tests typically include a hormonal profile, thyroid function, prolactin, and markers of
    insulin resistance and blood sugar. Rapid onset or fast progression prompts urgent
    investigation for other causes of androgen excess.
  </p>

  <h2>Treatment</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Acne</strong> — topical retinoids, and anti-androgens such as spironolactone or a suitable combined oral contraceptive where hormonal drive is clear</li>
    <li><strong>Hirsutism</strong> — laser hair reduction is the most effective option, with maintenance sessions; topical eflornithine slows regrowth on the face</li>
    <li><strong>Scalp thinning</strong> — topical minoxidil, with anti-androgens where appropriate</li>
    <li><strong>Acanthosis nigricans</strong> — improves mainly through addressing insulin resistance; topical retinoids help the texture somewhat</li>
    <li><strong>Metformin and lifestyle measures</strong> — managed with your physician or gynaecologist, and they improve the skin signs alongside everything else</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Why this is worth diagnosing properly</h3>
    <p>
      PCOS carries longer-term implications for diabetes risk, cardiovascular health and
      fertility. Treating the acne and the facial hair without anyone naming the underlying
      condition means those go unaddressed for years. If the pattern fits, it is worth
      investigating rather than treating each sign in isolation.
    </p>
  </div>
